CS447 FALL 2026 P1 : Online Course Registration System
======================================================
Author: [Johanna Schmidt]
Date: [10/8/26]

Overview:
 Basic implementation of a client/server application using the socket interface. The application models a university course registration system.

Directory Structure of the Tarball:
  p1/
    |- README: This file, containing project documentation and instructions.
    |- server.cpp: The implemented server application code.
    |- p1_helper.cpp: The implemented database helper functions.
    |- Makefile: Script to compile the server application.

Compilation:
  The original Makefile has not changed. Compilation is done by: g++=c++20 -Wall -pthread server.cpp p1_helper.cpp -o server. 
  
  How to Run:
  1. Start the server: ./server server.conf
  2. Connect a client using netcat: nc <server-ip> <server-port>
*all testing was done via PORT 3258. The server was ran through the zone server instance 0.

Notes and Design Choices:
 The most major design decision made was in regards to GradeRecord. It was discussed many times in class how some parts of GradeRecords should be implemented - such as random grade assignments, removal after dropping a course, and handling prereqs when courses are dropped. Some form of struct, Gamage perfers hashmaps, was to be used to handle grades. In order to track both grades and enrolled courses per student, I used an unordered_map to look up student records, by username, where alphabetical order was not necessary. Random grade generation happens at the top of server.cpp.
  I track student grades & registration through the ServerState sturct (contains the mutex, GradeRecords, and course catalog (unordered map)) which is shared across all clients. Because get_student_grades() takes only a username and, even when considering a thread_local approach (grade data lives in p1_helper), persistence fails with the mutex/thread I had set up as each new connection would create a new thread, thus losing the history. I instead kept my current mutex and ServerState, implemented the grade retrieval function within server.cpp, and left get_student_grades unmodified. 
  For mode switching, I created an enum class Mode with four constants: none, catalog, enrollment, and mycourses. This was my first time using an enum class and I was very pleased. Especially in a project like this, having four different booleans to switch between creates ample space for spelling errors and bugs. 
